{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"S6 (0d76-ebbf-ee3a-48ac) — CORRECTED VERDICT under the rescoped criteria. This SUPERSEDES my\nearlier comment on this epic, which said Bedrock did not clear the bar and that S8 must not cut\nover. Recorded as a COMMENT, not a description edit, for the sequencing reason the AC states.\n\nTHE RESCOPE. The operator ruled that LLM non-determinism is expected and that comparing two\nMODELS' judgements is OUT OF SCOPE for this epic. The epic's scope is that the same PAYLOADS\nreach the LLM and the same TOOLS are available, regardless of model and provider. Under that\nscope S6 PASSES.\n\nWHAT IS PROVEN, from captured outbound requests rather than from reading code (both class slots,\nzero network calls, zero tokens — the production path is aborted at the provider boundary):\n  * PAYLOAD PARITY — system prompt, messages, max_tokens and temperature are equivalent across\n    providers; the model id is the only differing field. The 8 provider-required envelope\n    differences are enumerated.\n  * TOOL PARITY — 6 tools, identical names, descriptions and input schemas.\n  * CAPABILITY DIFFERENCES — one, explicit and symmetric: `temperature` is withdrawn from opus\n    on BOTH providers and sent to sonnet on BOTH, driven by the model's capability record, not\n    by the provider.\n  * PROVIDER-CLEAN ROUTING — zero non-Bedrock calls across 84/84 v2 case-runs per slot.\n  * INVOKABILITY — both Bedrock inference-profile ids invoke in 896586841071/us-east-1.\n\nAll four are regression-gated by offline tests in `tests/unit/test_provider_parity.py`, not just\nobserved once.\n\nNOW INFORMATIONAL, NOT GATING: the paired-run ParityReport's decision flips (1 standard, 2\nfrontier) and the 0.929 frontier verdict agreement. They compare two different models'\njudgements. Worth keeping for S8's benefit: all three flips ran in BEDROCK's favour against the\ngold labels, validity was 1.000 on 360 case-runs, and the runtime error rate was 0.000.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR S8 (eb6e): the cutover is NOT blocked by S6. Two measured facts should travel\nwith it — Bedrock's `cachePoint` cache marker carries no TTL where Anthropic's `cache_control`\ncarries one (latent today, rebar sets no non-default TTL), and per-case-run cost is far above\nwhat the S6 plan assumed because the code-review arm is agentic (~100k input tokens, ~8.6\nprovider requests per case-run). Latency is at or better than direct Anthropic.\n"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785725865511099001,"uuid":"2d97a47b-d419-45d3-a6f1-c5bae53a9c13"}